  <GuideSection id="what-are-indices-faq">
    <MarketFAQ title="What are Indices - FAQ" columns={1} align="left" items={[
      { q: 'What is a stock market index?', a: 'A stock market index is a statistical measure that tracks the performance of a specific group of stocks, representing a particular market or sector. It provides a snapshot of market health and serves as a benchmark for investors.' },
      { q: 'What are the most popular stock market indices to trade?', a: 'Some of the most popular indices globally include the S&P 500, Dow Jones Industrial Average (DJIA), Nasdaq 100, FTSE 100, DAX 40, and Nikkei 225. These indices offer high liquidity and broad market exposure.' },
      { q: 'What is the difference between trading individual stocks and trading indices?', a: 'Trading individual stocks exposes you to the specific risks of a single company. Trading indices allows you to speculate on the collective performance of a basket of stocks, offering built-in diversification and generally smoother price action.' },
      { q: 'What factors influence the movement of stock market indices?', a: 'Index movements are driven by the performance of their constituent stocks, which in turn are affected by macroeconomic data (inflation, interest rates), corporate earnings reports, geopolitical events, and overall market sentiment.' },
    ]} />
  </GuideSection>
